\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{An interaction on a selected background}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{An \newvocab{interacting selected background} consists of a
\vocabk{selected Gaussian background}{fdip-005A} #{b=(\Pi,w,m,P)}, a real
polynomial #{\mathcal V:H\to\mathbb R}, and a formal variable
#{\lambda}. Thus the precision #{Q>0}, metric and selector are
supplied along with the interaction. Write #{X=\operatorname{im}P}
and #{Y=\ker P}, so #{H=X\mathbin\perp Y}.}

\p{For every #{x\in X}, define a probability law on #{Y} by
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_b(x)&=\int_Y e^{-\frac12\langle x+y,Q(x+y)\rangle}\,dy,\\
\kappa_{b,x}(dy)
 &=Z_b(x)^{-1}e^{-\frac12\langle x+y,Q(x+y)\rangle}\,dy.
\end{aligned}}
The volume #{dy} is the \vocabk{induced Euclidean volume}{fdip-002M}.
Positive precision makes #{Z_b(x)} positive and finite. The
[square-completion formula](fdip-000D) shows that this is a Gaussian
law whose mean depends linearly on #{x}. It is the conditional law
of the eliminated field given the retained value, using the
canonical density formula at every #{x}; general regular conditional
laws are unique only almost everywhere.}

\p{The \vocabk{fiber effective interaction}{fdip-0046} is the formal series
##{\begin{aligned}
M_{b,\mathcal V}(\lambda,x)
 &=\sum_{n\ge0}\frac{(-\lambda)^n}{n!}
       \int_Y\mathcal V(x+y)^n\,\kappa_{b,x}(dy),\\
F_{b,\mathcal V}(\lambda,x)&=-\log M_{b,\mathcal V}(\lambda,x).
\end{aligned}}
Each coefficient is a polynomial on #{X}: expand the polynomial
about the conditional mean and use the fixed centered Gaussian
moments. These moments are finite by
[polynomial integrability](fdip-002W). The series #{M} has constant
coefficient one, so its logarithm is a formal power series in
#{\lambda} with coefficients in the [real polynomial algebra](fdip-002V)
#{\mathcal P(X)} on #{X}. This extends the
[quartic construction](fdip-0046) to any polynomial interaction and
any orthogonal selector. Constants in #{x} remain part of #{F}.}

\p{This definition is coefficientwise. It imposes no positivity on
#{\mathcal V}, asserts no convergence for nonzero #{\lambda}, and
uses no interchange of an infinite sum with an integral. The notation
#{O(\lambda^k)} means divisibility by #{\lambda^k}. A fiber logarithm
is different from the Legendre transform that defines a one-particle
irreducible effective action in
\citet{v2, Proposition 3.23, p. 50}{etingof2024mathematical}. Here the
retained field is fixed while its orthogonal complement is integrated.}
